From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 05:21:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 030/184] segacd: look for the transfer where it would actually
 happen

Hardware reported the transfer-status bits as 8 and A alternating: a
status packet arrives, one is sometimes in flight, and the bit that
says a command went out is never set.

That measurement had a hole in it. The register was sampled at the top
of the interrupt -- before the write that is supposed to start a
transfer, and a packet period after the last one -- so a bit that
rises and falls in between was never observable. It is now polled
immediately after the write, which is the only place the answer could
have been.

The previous commit claimed to pulse HOCK and did not: the register
already held that bit, and writing a set bit again produces no edge.
If it really is a strobe, it needs taking low first, which also writes
zero to the two abort bits and is therefore only safe when neither
transfer is running. That goes behind holding B, since clearing the
bit that started the link is the one action here that might end it.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 37 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 286cf1a..0d6f817 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -144,6 +144,7 @@ static UBYTE subvbl;            /* the sub CPU's own heartbeat */
 static UBYTE cdd_ctrl;          /* DTS/DRS as last sampled */
 static UBYTE cdd_ctrl_ever;     /* DTS/DRS ever seen set at all */
 static UBYTE cdd_blocked;       /* interrupts spent waiting for DTS/DRS */
+static UBYTE cdd_hock_edge;     /* holding B: take HOCK low to make an edge */
 
 void segacd_cdd_init(void)
 {
@@ -169,6 +170,7 @@ void segacd_cdd_init(void)
         } while ((f & 0xFF00) != 0x5A00 && --spin);
 
         cdd_cmd_on = (UBYTE)((f & 1) ? 0 : 1);
+        cdd_hock_edge = (UBYTE)((f & 2) ? 1 : 0);
     }
 
     /* Vector first, then unmask, then start the clock: the first packet
@@ -198,11 +200,36 @@ static void cdd_command(const UBYTE *nib)       /* nine nibbles */
     w[3] = (UWORD)((nib[6] << 8) | nib[7]);
     w[4] = (UWORD)((nib[8] << 8) | ((~sum) & 0x0F));
 
-    /* And now start the transfer. Filling the registers is not sending
-     * them: the drive answered "invalid" to every question including
-     * the one that asks for nothing, which is what a drive says when it
-     * is reporting on its own and never heard the question at all. */
-    *CDD_CTRL = CDD_HOCK;
+    /* HOCK is documented as starting a communication, so on hardware
+     * that reads as a strobe -- but the register already holds it, and
+     * writing a bit that is already set produces no edge at all. The
+     * first attempt at "pulsing" it did exactly that and changed
+     * nothing, which is its own small lesson. A real edge means taking
+     * it low first, and taking it low also writes 0 to DTS and DRS,
+     * which is documented as aborting a transfer -- harmless only
+     * because the caller has just checked that neither is running.
+     *
+     * Gated on holding B, because clearing the bit that started the
+     * link is the one action here that could plausibly end it. */
+    if (cdd_hock_edge) {
+        *CDD_CTRL = 0;
+        *CDD_CTRL = CDD_HOCK;
+    }
+
+    /* Now watch for the transfer actually starting. The bit was
+     * previously only ever sampled at the top of the interrupt --
+     * which is to say, before the write that was supposed to set it,
+     * and a whole packet period after the last one. "Never observed"
+     * meant nothing at all; this is where it has to be looked for. */
+    {
+        UWORD n;
+        for (n = 0; n < 512; n++) {
+            UBYTE c = *CDD_CTRL_RD;
+            cdd_ctrl_ever |= (UBYTE)(c & (CDD_DTS | CDD_DRS));
+            if (c & CDD_DTS)
+                break;
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 /* Ask the drive something, cycling through the questions.
